\newcommand{\Fp}{\mathbb{F}_p}
\newcommand{\Zp}{\mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}}
\newcommand{\Hom}{\operatorname{Hom}}

\title{Sharpness of Finite-Group Cocycle Averaging\\in Prime Characteristic}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 1, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let a finite group $G$ act linearly on a vector space $V$ over a field $k$. If $|G|$ is invertible in $k$, averaging shows that every degree-one cocycle $c:G\to V$ is a coboundary. We prove the sharpness of this hypothesis by a uniform counterexample in every prime characteristic. For each prime $p$, take the additive cyclic group $\Zp$, written multiplicatively, acting trivially on $V=\Fp$. Its coordinate map satisfies the cocycle identity, but every coboundary for the trivial action is zero while the coordinate map is not. Moreover, $|G|=p=0$ in $\Fp$. Thus unconditional degree-one averaging is false when the characteristic may divide the group order. We give complete proofs, computational illustrations, and consequences for continuous cohomology and arithmetic geometry.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Averaging over a finite group is a basic method for producing invariant objects. One sums data over a group orbit and divides by the group order. Multiplication by a group element merely permutes the summands, so the normalized sum is invariant. This principle underlies invariant metrics, equivariant projections, fixed-point arguments, and cohomological vanishing.

Division by $|G|$ is not always possible. If the coefficient field has characteristic dividing $|G|$, the group order is zero in that field. It is then natural to ask whether only the familiar proof fails, or whether its conclusion is false. For degree-one group cohomology, the latter occurs.

For every prime $p$, we exhibit a cyclic group of order $p$ acting trivially on the one-dimensional space $\Fp$, together with a nonzero cocycle. Since every coboundary for a trivial action is zero, this cocycle determines a nontrivial cohomology class. The group order simultaneously vanishes in the coefficient field. This elementary family identifies the exact obstruction that prime-to-characteristic hypotheses exclude.

\section{Cocycles, coboundaries, and affine actions}

\begin{definition}
Let $G$ be a group and $V$ a vector space over a field $k$. A \emph{linear action} assigns to each $g\in G$ an invertible $k$-linear map $v\mapsto g\cdot v$ such that $e\cdot v=v$ and $(gh)\cdot v=g\cdot(h\cdot v)$. The action is \emph{trivial} if $g\cdot v=v$ for all $g$ and $v$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{degree-one cocycle} is a function $c:G\to V$ satisfying
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cocycle}
c(gh)=c(g)+g\cdot c(h)
\end{equation}
for all $g,h\in G$. A \emph{degree-one coboundary} is a function of the form
\begin{equation}\label{eq:coboundary}
(\delta v)(g)=v-g\cdot v
\end{equation}
for some $v\in V$.
\end{definition}

Coboundaries are cocycles, since
\[
v-(gh)\cdot v=(v-g\cdot v)+g\cdot(v-h\cdot v).
\]
Writing $Z^1(G,V)$ for cocycles and $B^1(G,V)$ for coboundaries, first cohomology is
\[
H^1(G,V)=Z^1(G,V)/B^1(G,V).
\]

The definitions have a geometric interpretation. A cocycle determines an affine action
\[
g\star x=g\cdot x+c(g).
\]
Equation~\eqref{eq:cocycle} is exactly the associativity condition for this action. A cocycle is a coboundary precisely when the affine action has a fixed point: if $c(g)=v-g\cdot v$, then $g\star v=v$. Thus $H^1$ measures translational displacement that cannot be removed by changing the origin.

For a trivial action, the cocycle law becomes $c(gh)=c(g)+c(h)$, so cocycles are homomorphisms to the additive group of $V$. Meanwhile every coboundary vanishes because $v-g\cdot v=v-v=0$.

\section{Averaging when the group order is invertible}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-group averaging in degree one]\label{thm:averaging}
Let a finite group $G$ act linearly on a vector space $V$ over a field $k$. If $|G|$ is invertible in $k$, every degree-one cocycle $c:G\to V$ is a coboundary. Consequently $H^1(G,V)=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Define
\[
w=\frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{h\in G}c(h).
\]
For fixed $g$, the cocycle identity gives $g\cdot c(h)=c(gh)-c(g)$. Therefore
\[
\sum_{h\in G}g\cdot c(h)
=\sum_{h\in G}c(gh)-|G|c(g)
=\sum_{h\in G}c(h)-|G|c(g),
\]
because $h\mapsto gh$ permutes $G$. Dividing by $|G|$ gives $g\cdot w=w-c(g)$, and hence $c(g)=w-g\cdot w$. Thus $c=\delta w$.
\end{proof}

The theorem suggests a tempting but false strengthening: that every degree-one cocycle of every finite linear action might be a coboundary even when $|G|$ is zero in the coefficient field. The next sections disprove this uniformly.

\section{The cyclic coordinate cocycle}

Fix a prime $p$. Let $G_p$ be the additive group of $\Zp$, but write its operation multiplicatively. Thus if elements $g$ and $h$ have additive coordinates $[a]$ and $[b]$, their product $gh$ has coordinate $[a+b]$. The group $G_p$ is cyclic of order $p$.

Let $V_p=\Fp$, viewed as a one-dimensional vector space over itself, and give it the trivial action
\[
g\cdot x=x.
\]
Define the \emph{coordinate map} $c_p:G_p\to V_p$ by sending an element to its underlying residue coordinate.

\begin{lemma}[Coordinate cocycle law]\label{lem:cocycle}
For all $g,h\in G_p$,
\[
c_p(gh)=c_p(g)+g\cdot c_p(h).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $g$ and $h$ have coordinates $[a]$ and $[b]$, then $gh$ has coordinate $[a+b]$. Hence
\[
c_p(gh)=[a+b]=[a]+[b]=c_p(g)+c_p(h).
\]
The action is trivial, so $g\cdot c_p(h)=c_p(h)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Trivial-action coboundaries]\label{lem:coboundaryzero}
If a group acts trivially on a vector space, every degree-one coboundary is identically zero.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For every $v$ and $g$, equation~\eqref{eq:coboundary} becomes $(\delta v)(g)=v-v=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Nonvanishing]\label{lem:nonzero}
The coordinate cocycle $c_p$ is nonzero.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The element with additive coordinate $[1]$ is sent to $[1]$. Since $p$ is prime, $p\geq2$, and $[1]\neq[0]$ in $\Fp$.
\end{proof}

\section{The sharpness theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Non-coboundary theorem]\label{thm:noncoboundary}
For every prime $p$, the coordinate cocycle $c_p:G_p\to\Fp$ is not a coboundary.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:coboundaryzero}, every coboundary for this action is zero. By Lemma~\ref{lem:nonzero}, $c_p$ is not zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Vanishing of the group order]\label{lem:order}
The group $G_p$ has cardinality $p$, and the image of $|G_p|$ in $\Fp$ is zero.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
There are exactly $p$ residue classes modulo $p$. By characteristic $p$, the sum of $p$ copies of $1$ is zero in $\Fp$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform counterexample to unconditional averaging]\label{thm:main}
For every prime $p$, there are a finite group $G_p$, a one-dimensional $\Fp$-vector space $V_p$, a linear action, and a function $c_p:G_p\to V_p$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $|G_p|=p$ and its image in $\Fp$ is zero;
\item $c_p(gh)=c_p(g)+g\cdot c_p(h)$ for every $g,h\in G_p$;
\item no $v\in V_p$ satisfies $c_p(g)=v-g\cdot v$ for every $g\in G_p$.
\end{enumerate}
One may take $G_p$ cyclic of order $p$, $V_p=\Fp$, the action trivial, and $c_p$ the coordinate map.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Property (i) is Lemma~\ref{lem:order}, property (ii) is Lemma~\ref{lem:cocycle}, and property (iii) is Theorem~\ref{thm:noncoboundary}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nonvanishing first cohomology]
For every prime $p$,
\[
H^1(G_p,\Fp)\cong\Hom(\Zp,\Fp^+)\cong\Fp,
\]
so in particular $H^1(G_p,\Fp)\neq0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
For the trivial action, cocycles are additive homomorphisms and coboundaries vanish. A homomorphism from a cyclic group is determined by the image of a generator, which may be any element of $\Fp$. The coordinate cocycle corresponds to the value $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Sharpness of averaging]
The invertibility hypothesis in Theorem~\ref{thm:averaging} cannot be removed from a general degree-one vanishing theorem. Every positive prime characteristic has a finite counterexample whose group order is zero in the field.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms and examples}

The construction can be illustrated exhaustively. Represent both $G_p$ and $\Fp$ by the integers $0,\ldots,p-1$. For every ordered pair $(a,b)$, compare
\[
c_p((a+b)\bmod p)
\quad\text{with}\quad
(c_p(a)+c_p(b))\bmod p.
\]
There are $p^2$ pairs, so verification uses $O(p^2)$ modular operations and constant auxiliary space when streamed. To enumerate coboundaries, test each $v\in\Fp$ and each group element $a$; every table obtained is the zero table. This literal enumeration also costs $O(p^2)$ operations, although the identity $v-v=0$ reduces the mathematical argument to a constant-time witness $c_p(1)=1$.

For $p=5$, the coordinate table is
\[
(0,1,2,3,4).
\]
For instance, $3+4\equiv2\pmod5$, and
\[
c_5(3+4)=c_5(2)=2\equiv3+4=c_5(3)+c_5(4)\pmod5.
\]
Every coboundary table is $(0,0,0,0,0)$, while $5\bmod5=0$. The same computation applies to every prime.

\section{Geometric and profinite interpretation}

The cocycle defines the affine translation action
\[
g\star x=x+c_p(g)
\]
on the affine line over $\Fp$. It has no global fixed point: the element with coordinate $1$ sends $x$ to $x+1$, which cannot equal $x$. Theorem~\ref{thm:averaging}, by contrast, says that when $|G|$ is invertible, orbit averaging supplies a fixed point for every affine action with linear part prescribed by $G$. The example therefore marks a fixed-point boundary as well as a cohomological one.

A profinite group is an inverse limit of finite groups. Continuous maps into finite discrete modules commonly factor through finite quotients. If a profinite group is pro-prime-to-$\ell$, the orders of its finite quotients are prime to $\ell$ and therefore invertible in $\mathbb{F}_\ell$. A continuous cocycle that factors through such a quotient may be averaged there and then lifted. This motivates vanishing results for first continuous cohomology under suitable hypotheses.

The counterexample explains why prime-to-$\ell$ is necessary in general. Pro-$\ell$ groups naturally admit quotients of order $\ell$, and the coordinate cocycle on such a quotient supplies a finite nonvanishing class. Pullback along a quotient map can transport the obstruction to the profinite setting.

These distinctions enter arithmetic geometry. Étale cohomology of affine curves uses cohomological-dimension and finiteness statements for geometric fundamental groups. Completed group algebras such as $\mathbb{Z}_\ell[[\Gamma]]$ encode continuous actions over infinite towers, while compact Nakayama theory relates modules to their reductions modulo $\ell$. Selmer structures impose compatible local conditions on global classes, and Pontryagin duality converts cohomological information into module structure. In deformation theory, $H^1$ commonly describes infinitesimal deformations and $H^2$ their obstructions. At every stage, an averaging argument must track whether finite quotient orders are invertible in the coefficients.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The family has four useful properties. It is uniform in every prime, minimal in dimension and group order, transparent because its cocycle law is modular addition, and aligned exactly with the failure of division by $|G|$. It does not assert that modular cohomology is always nonzero; special actions or additional hypotheses may still force vanishing. It instead disproves any universal theorem that omits a condition excluding the cyclic prime-order example.

A next step is to develop continuous crossed homomorphisms into finite discrete modules and prove finite-quotient factorization. Finite averaging can then be transferred across quotients whose orders are prime to the coefficient characteristic. The complementary pro-$p$ theory should track how cyclic coordinate classes inflate to nonvanishing continuous classes.

Further work may connect these statements to étale cohomology of affine curves, completed group algebras, compact Nakayama theory, and criteria relating finite reduction modulo $\ell$ to torsion and vanishing of the Iwasawa $\mu$-invariant. Cartesian Selmer structures and Pontryagin duals can assemble finite-level control into results over infinite extensions. Finally, obstruction theory for framed and unframed deformation functors can use degree-two vanishing to establish formal smoothness and power-series presentations of deformation rings.

\section{Conclusion}

For every prime $p$, the cyclic group of order $p$ acting trivially on $\Fp$ has a coordinate cocycle that is not a coboundary, while its group order is zero in the coefficient field. This uniform construction disproves unconditional finite-group cocycle averaging and proves that the prime-to-characteristic hypothesis marks a genuine mathematical boundary. Its simplicity makes it a canonical finite test case, while its structure identifies the obstruction that must be excluded in continuous cohomology and arithmetic applications.

\end{document}

